#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148270} :activity/id #uuid "66824b85-374f-5412-9f2f-47a7d45ad385" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048995 :topic/id #uuid "d1edd866-fc11-5971-b454-c205e7356f7f" :topic/math-academy-id 2464} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148795 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t),$ in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18.$ This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{t} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{t}}{2} & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{t}}{\\cancel{2}} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3,$ because $9=3^2.$ So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." :tutorial/id #uuid "35ba03ad-41cc-5f76-9618-f78f0e3c0529" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8849 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87338de0-96ec-5a69-a9d2-940674b36916" :step/math-academy-id 196865 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147176 :question/id #uuid "94d6edd2-52f9-581a-84f0-3003ccbaadd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7328749-7ecf-5040-9d18-e9274187d485" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497357}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Time"} :step/id #uuid "7f05b5aa-3eba-5e22-981c-ba97b773c331" :step/math-academy-id 196866 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147177 :question/id #uuid "4a2c8602-04d5-5ec7-846f-2647249994fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "363026fc-b4ac-5b35-8825-717a113df12b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048302}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497384}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Model and Solving for an Unknown Time"} :step/id #uuid "76f4ea70-5d3e-59c6-ab03-6093273dafcf" :step/math-academy-id 196869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147178 :question/id #uuid "9759efbf-fdb7-595c-822d-85c6b5f3549e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14271"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08d13ef7-5569-5cc4-9692-9ed99a3be3e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497363}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating and Solving a Model Given Some Percentage Growth"} :step/id #uuid "07e18a18-e0f7-514d-9a1d-5e49ae4b0b94" :step/math-academy-id 196872 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148796 :tutorial/content "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that $N(5) = 5\\,000,$ So let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}(1.2)^{5}}{(1.2)^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}}{\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & ={N}_{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with. \n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009.\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae3d753f-4a2d-55b0-9fb7-a3f746f66018" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8854 :tutorial/title "Unknown Initial Values"} :step/id #uuid "da8a83fe-da24-58c8-be84-aad5b70f01a0" :step/math-academy-id 196875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147179 :question/id #uuid "07a30fba-ad4b-56a0-b6ce-21941d106b38" :question/math-academy-id "e-14276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c127787-8a31-524b-8b7d-68c15b714335" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497382}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Initial Value"} :step/id #uuid "20edaa81-4eeb-5d4b-98a7-837cb86e910d" :step/math-academy-id 196876}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Growth Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147664}]}
